<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch7-section-thermodynamics-review-subsection-standard-atmosphere" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Standard atmosphere</title>

    <p>
        The American Meteorological Society (AMS)
        <url href="https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Standard_atmosphere">defines</url>
        a <term>standard atmosphere</term> as a
        "hypothetical vertical distribution of atmospheric temperature, pressure and density
        that, by international agreement, is taken to be representative of the atmosphere."
        A standard atmosphere is subject to the assumptions that the air can be treated as an ideal gas and is in hydrostatic balance
        (at least at lower altitudes).
        Some standard atmospheres<mdash />including the U.S. Standard Atmosphere<mdash />are
        further subject to the assumptions that the air is dry and homogeneously mixed
        (at least at lower altitudes)
        and the atmosphere rotates along with Earth,
        is horizontally homogeneous,
        is calm,
        and is a representative average over the diurnal and seasonal cycles.
    </p>

    <p>
        Several common<mdash />and nearly identical<mdash />standard atmospheres apply to locations in the middle latitudes,
        including the International Organization for Standardization (ISO) <url href="https://www.iso.org/standard/7472.html">International Standard Atmosphere</url> (ISA);
        the <url href="https://www.ngdc.noaa.gov/stp/space-weather/online-publications/miscellaneous/us-standard-atmosphere-1976/us-standard-atmosphere_st76-1562_noaa.pdf">U.S. Standard Atmosphere</url>;
        and the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO) <url href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19930083952">Standard Atmosphere</url>.
        The U.S. Standard Atmosphere extends through the largest depth
        (<m>z_{min} = -5 \, \text{km}</m>, <m>z_{max} = 1000 \, \text{km}</m>)
        and is largely consistent with other commonly used standard atmospheres,
        and therefore it is chosen for brief presentation here.
    </p>

    <p>
        The lower portion
        (through <m>z = 130 \, \text{km}</m>)
        of the U.S. Standard Atmosphere's vertical temperature profile is shown in <xref ref="ch7-standard-atmosphere" text="type-global" /> below.
        Differing temperature behaviors lead to the identification of
        four main layer<mdash />the troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, and thermosphere<mdash />and
        three isothermal layers called "pauses"<mdash />the tropopause, stratopause, and mesopause.
    </p>

    <p>
        Notice temperature on average cools with increasing height in the troposphere,
        at <m>6.5\text{°C} \, \text{km}^{-1}</m> according to the U.S. Standard Atmosphere.
        The troposphere is the atmospheric layer in which nearly all weather occurs
        and in which we spend all (or, if we fly, nearly all) of our time.
        This average temperature behavior in the troposphere motivates the definition of a lapse rate
        as a rate of temperature <em>decrease</em> with increasing height.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch7-standard-atmosphere">
        <caption>
            The golden curve shows the vertical temperature profile of the U.S. Standard Atmosphere through the lowest 130 km of Earth's atmosphere.
            Layers are labeled.
            The figure has been modified from
            NOAA JetStream's <url href="https://www.noaa.gov/jetstream/atmosphere/layers-of-atmosphere">Layers of the Atmosphere</url>
            (in the public domain)
            with the addition of gray shading to show the vertical extents of the pauses.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch7-standard-atmosphere.png" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>
                Diagram of the layers of Earth's atmosphere with their names and vertical temperature profiles.
                according to the U.S. Standard Atmosphere.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>

</subsection>